  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 119 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nAnother active day is expected on Sunday. Post frontal upslope will \nbe present over the eastern plains, with brisk west winds aloft. \nMeanwhile, the passing upper trough will lift through the area \nduring the afternoon, giving us everything we need for some strong \nto severe storms. Models indicate a good fetch of CAPE across the \neastern plains, approaching 1000 J/kg and increasing a bit further \neast. With flow aloft turning more northwesterly, 40-45 knots of \neffective shear is well within expectations as well. As the upper \nlow moves past, another cold front will also dive south over the \nplains, giving plenty of low-level forcing for storms to fire and \nintensify. Best chance for severe storms will be along this front, \nwhich will first hit the Palmer Divide during the mid-late \nafternoon, pushing south over the eastern plains into Sunday Night. \nOnce the front passes through, convection will weaken as the best \nforcing departs our area, leaving some lingering rain showers into \nMonday morning over the southeast plains.\n\nStill a good bit of variation in the long-term guidance regarding \nnext week. Still expecting a closed upper low to move onto the CA \ncoast, pushing east towards the middle of next week. Beyond that, \nmodels begin to diverge greatly, moving the wave at different speeds \nand with different tracks, which will have palpable impacts on how \nour local weather evolves over the next week. That being said, NBM \nconsensus has leaned towards and overall cooler and wetter pattern \nfor our area, particularly Tuesday through Thursday over portions of \nour southern mountains. Meanwhile, temperatures will remain near or \njust below seasonal levels.",
